Yeah. Like if you had your, uh, cleric as the first party member, uh, they would, that's a, they

00:34:43.180 --> 00:34:44.380
have a bad time. Yeah.

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:50.060
They would be screwed. Uh, but there are spells that the, uh, the clerics and the mage can cast.

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:56.480
And it's a little like, it's a little tedious that the, the game explains none of them that

00:34:56.480 --> 00:35:01.000
you constantly have to be checking in with your, uh, manual to understand them because they're

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:03.480
not even like, I'm sure.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:04.980
They don't even sound like it. Right.

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:08.420
I'm sure that they are something, maybe it's Latin or something.

00:35:08.760 --> 00:35:09.920
Maybe, maybe, I don't know.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:10.860
I'm making that up, but like.

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:13.360
It's some magic, it's a magic language.

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:23.000
Yeah. So we have, uh, you know, cleric spells like Dios, Milwa, Kalfo, Manifo, um, Bamatu.

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:30.060
All these things are like, I'm, I'm going to remember Dios after I see what it does, right?

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:35.800
Like it heals, uh, HP, which is incredibly important and can be done outside of battle.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:39.520
Uh, probably unfortunately only once the first time you have it.

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:41.820
Another charge based magic system.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:43.240
Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:45.760
Um, and yeah, different levels to that.

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:49.020
But like, that's not, that's not necessarily to complain.

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:55.180
I think it's great that, uh, you can have a party that does so many different things to

00:35:55.180 --> 00:36:00.700
the point where like, yeah, most combat is just going to be, um, you know, fight, fight,

00:36:00.900 --> 00:36:02.380
fight, fight, parry, parry, parry.

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:08.820
Yeah. Right. Um, that's what it's going to break down to, but there's a lot of like post decision

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:12.020
about like, okay, well, do I use my cleric now?

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:14.060
Do I try to take on another battle?

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:16.020
Do I risk running into a trap?

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:20.840
Like, uh, you know, do my mage has powerful spells?

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:24.680
Is it, is this a strong enough enemy that now it's time to use this?

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:29.640
It is interesting that that's kind of how, you know, like this game is so, I don't want

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:33.740
to say resource intensive, but like, you know, you really have to think about, oh,

00:36:33.740 --> 00:36:36.000
no, this is a resource management game for sure.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:39.760
Okay, cool. So like, you really have to think about like, when's the best time to do these things?

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:42.620
Cause you really only get like one or two uses out of it.

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:50.500
Uh, as a result, I felt like this was a much more interesting system than it presented itself

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:54.400
to be. You know, even though I said, it's mostly fight, fight, fight, parry, parry, parry.

00:36:54.400 --> 00:37:01.160
Uh, you know, that's because if you're fighting slime or skeletons, like that's, that's what

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:04.120
the combat's going to be because it's just an ordinary encounter.

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:08.940
And then you have these extraordinary encounters where all of a sudden it's an enemy you've

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:10.240
never seen before.

00:37:10.660 --> 00:37:15.460
Uh, you, you know, you haven't encountered, you're not going to run into over and over again

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:17.220
like you would in a JRPG.

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:21.100
And you have to kind of just like figure it out or die.

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:29.680
Yeah. I think the one thing that I didn't appreciate about this system is that they're like, you

00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:34.640
can have your, you're going to run into people and it's going to show you, oh, you ran into

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:39.900
these guys. And then it'll just like, oh, you, there's also these guys behind them.

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:45.720
And you didn't know that when you first got into this encounter.

00:37:45.720 --> 00:37:52.860
So you could have like two, two groups of five, like, uh, what are they called?

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:55.460
Uh, not brandishers.

00:37:56.260 --> 00:37:58.940
Uh, basically bandits.

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:04.080
And then you'd get like five kobolds behind them, but you only knew about the bandits and

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:08.080
it can really, it feels like it's really mean.

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:13.800
And I understand that like, you know, difficulty's fun, but it just, I would like more information.

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:20.840
I think that if there was more information presented, uh, then I could, I could get down

00:38:20.840 --> 00:38:22.820
with the difficulty more.

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:23.940
...

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:30.700
...

00:38:31.680 --> 00:38:33.080
...

00:38:39.260 --> 00:40:19.960
Thank you. And another thing that's interesting about combat with these enemies is when you are more leveled up and a braver warrior, a lot of these enemies know they're outmatched.

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:21.600
Yeah, they know they're outmatched.

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:24.200
And we know that there's built-in systems for this stuff.

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:29.540
We talked about a little bit on Final Fantasy that every enemy has a calculation that it runs to flee.

00:40:29.720 --> 00:40:32.220
But that doesn't happen as often as it does here.

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:38.300
This really does feel like you kind of become a legend a little bit as you continue to level up.

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:42.320
And they're genuinely scared of you, so you just continue to move along.

00:41:42.320 --> 00:41:49.280
At least on the—again, I spent a lot of time on the first floor of this maze.

00:41:49.460 --> 00:41:53.200
So maybe this is just for, like, easy baby mode.

00:41:53.520 --> 00:41:59.440
No, no, no. I would just keep trying to disarm it, and then it would eventually get disarmed.

00:42:00.260 --> 00:42:04.360
Well, no, I think you taught me something there because, see, I was reading it different.

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:07.920
It said—basically, the screen pops up and it says,

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:15.180
As a chest, you may—and then gives you the choice of inspect, calpho, disarm, open, leave alone.

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:22.060
And let's ignore calpho for a second and pretend that you didn't know that it's a spell that your cleric can use to—

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:25.280
Oh. It's a level two spell, though, so you won't have it right away.

00:42:25.420 --> 00:42:26.940
Yeah. I don't think so.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:31.320
I know, like, you can get certain good rolls or whatever, but I doubt that you would have that in your party.

00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:34.480
And that will detect the trap on the chest.

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:36.900
So calpho's great, but, like, you don't have that right away.

00:42:37.140 --> 00:42:43.280
But I read it as, like, it's a chest, and you may do, like, one of these things.

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:44.840
And I thought that, like—

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:46.780
That's the only one thing you can do?

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:51.760
Right, right. Like, inspecting it while it has a trap on it would be a bad idea.

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:55.220
Oh. And so you would move to disarm it first.

00:42:55.460 --> 00:42:56.380
But obviously, like—

00:42:56.380 --> 00:43:00.500
No, no, no, no. Yeah, now that you've explained it to me, I can see why that doesn't really even make sense.

00:43:00.500 --> 00:43:03.940
But, like, that's kind of the—that's what I made up in my head as the rules.

00:43:04.040 --> 00:43:04.840
So that's why I was like—

00:43:04.840 --> 00:43:07.640
And maybe on lower levels, like, the inspect doesn't work.

00:43:08.020 --> 00:43:09.400
So you might have to guess.

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:17.500
That's me guessing, though, because I never, you know, I never got to the part where—what's his name?

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:22.540
The mad wizard leaves you a note that says that the king sucks or anything like that.

00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:28.360
So maybe it gets harder and more random as the game goes on.

00:43:28.460 --> 00:43:31.460
But at least in the beginning, it's very easy.

00:43:31.460 --> 00:43:48.740
I think you just run the risk of if you use the wrong thing to disarm it, which can even happen apparently, you know, in the inspect column, you're, you know, whatever character you select, if they don't have maybe a high enough intelligence or something, they can select the wrong thing.

00:43:48.740 --> 00:44:01.840
You do run a chance that the thing that they choose to use to disarm it actually activates the trap and thus, like, poisons the entire party or deals damage to the character in front, you know, that kind of thing.

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:04.380
So they're not without risk.

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:15.220
And I think that is actually just a huge thing in general for the game is that it's a very deceptive dungeon design.

00:44:15.220 --> 00:44:21.720
So I think that the game as a whole, we were talking about the map and how, like, it's mostly uninteresting or whatever.

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:31.460
I think it is purposefully designed that way to mislead players with traps and more difficult monsters than they're prepared for.

00:44:31.700 --> 00:44:37.540
I do think that they leave a lot of long hallways that you're like, oh, well, I wonder what's down this corridor.

00:44:37.540 --> 00:44:49.040
Like, I think they're trying to warn you to not necessarily be exploration focused and kind of stay on the path and go in there with a goal because otherwise they will punish you.

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:57.020
Yeah. But I also think, like, you're not, you're not gonna, I mean, you're gonna have a map that you have drawn to your best extent.

00:44:58.100 --> 00:45:01.920
But, you know, to go any further, you basically have to explore.

00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:11.240
So I think this is more just like, here, use this party, like, the party that you, that you boot up this game with, like, they're not gonna be the ones that complete the game.

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:15.700
If you are coming into this game blind, they're gonna die.

00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:21.180
They're probably, maybe, maybe one or two will survive if you've been very careful with them.

00:45:21.360 --> 00:45:26.400
But I think you're supposed to kind of just, like, poke and prod and learn.

00:45:26.400 --> 00:45:30.160
And now you know, this is what I gotta do with this enemy.

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:31.540
This is what I gotta do with this trap.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:33.160
This is what I gotta do on this level.

00:45:34.880 --> 00:45:40.400
And, oh, here's, this is where the, the secret wall door is.

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:47.120
And I think it is actually just saying, like, look, like, you know, there's no, there's no fail state.

00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:52.220
Like, you, you will fail a lot, but there's no end game because you game over.

00:45:53.500 --> 00:45:58.240
So just, just keep prodding and you will eventually get through it.

00:45:58.360 --> 00:46:02.040
This is a game about, uh, about attrition.

00:46:03.080 --> 00:46:05.800
Right, right, right. And I think, I think we're saying the same thing.

00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:07.280
Maybe I'm just being a little harder on them.

00:46:07.360 --> 00:46:10.440
But I, I'm, I'm saying it's part of the gameplay experience.

00:46:10.720 --> 00:46:14.300
They are telling you, you know, like, keep poking around.

00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:18.320
But at the same time, every chest usually has a trap on it.

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:25.900
Uh, every item requires you to identify it before using it because it might be a cursed item or something.

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:33.660
You know, uh, there, there's, there's just basically like a lot of things in the game that you potentially could see as a reward.

00:46:33.660 --> 00:46:38.340
Like a chest or an item that isn't actually beneficial to you.

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:47.780
And, uh, just, you know, was it, was an event or experience in the game that, uh, just will, it's not like a one-off thing.

00:46:47.880 --> 00:46:50.840
It'll just continue to happen as you go further along in the maze.

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:53.340
You, you'll continue to run into these kinds of things.

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:59.440
So there's not too much, uh, in the, in the way of like getting ahead in the game.

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:16.000
You do kind of just have to experience the game and, and, and literally gain experience and level up and create and get stronger characters to, uh, bring up that armor class and raise their HP and eventually promote their classes.

00:47:16.120 --> 00:47:21.740
Uh, which is like an interesting thing because you can even get some of those promoted classes.

00:47:21.740 --> 00:47:33.620
If you're lucky enough on your roles, which are, um, they're not Nobunaga's ambition style roles where it's like a number that flashes around and you hit like stop and hope that you get a good one.

00:47:33.740 --> 00:47:35.400
It's just generated for you.

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:42.280
And then you get a few extra stats that you can pump into it, but you can just start off with like a samurai.

00:47:42.500 --> 00:47:49.120
If you get a lucky enough stat based on like, if you selected, uh, I forget what it was.

00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:53.660
It's like, if you picked a dwarf, uh, maybe it was a gnome.

00:47:53.760 --> 00:48:03.720
If you picked a dwarf and a, um, and you picked good, there's a chance you'll, you'll get a good enough role to start as a samurai.

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:05.560
A dwarven samurai.

00:48:06.280 --> 00:48:08.300
Right, right. The best, the best kind.

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:18.760
Yeah. Um, it's just interesting that that can happen, but also you can just do it the traditional way too of eventually getting promoted to the class, which I think are some of the other ones like are just.

00:48:19.120 --> 00:48:22.580
Either they have like 1% odds or they're actually impossible.

00:48:22.960 --> 00:48:24.360
Like to be an evil ninja or something.

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:38.880
Right, right. Um, so all that in mind, there's very little way of getting ahead except for this one obvious exploit of, um, pooling and divvying the gold amongst your party.

00:48:38.880 --> 00:48:40.920
